[Unit]
Description=PokeDumpster catalog refresh (%i)
# pd-1uem item 5: LANDING AND DERIVING ARE TWO UNITS. This is the landing half —
# it fetches from the upstreams and, with landing on, puts every response in the
# bucket. pkdump-derive@%i is the deriving one.
#
# {{REPO_DIR}} is sed-substituted by deploy/units-lib.sh (single-clone
# deployment).
# Layer 2 (pokedumpster-ivq.3): push the journal tail to Pushover if a refresh
# run fails (this is a discrete oneshot, so OnFailure fires reliably here).
OnFailure=pkdump-alert@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# DeckDumpster split this into prices / sealed / edhrec timers; PokeDumpster
# collapses them to a single `pkdump data refresh`.
#
# ── IT RUNS IN ITS OWN CONTAINER, NOT THE SERVER'S (pd-kncd) ────────────────
# This was `podman exec systemd-pkdump-%i pkdump data refresh` until Aug 2026,
# and that call drops the environment: an exec'd process gets the CONTAINER's
# env plus explicit -e flags and nothing else. So a drop-in here setting
# `Environment=PKDUMP_LAND_RAW=1` — the documented way to turn landing on —
# reached this unit and never reached the refresh, producing a green nightly
# timer that landed nothing.
#
# -e would have fixed only half of it. The app container mounts the data volume
# and nothing else, `podman exec` cannot add a mount to a running container, and
# mounting the lake's credentials on the app container would hand the always-on
# web server ambient write access to the lake bucket. So the job runs in its own
# container from the same image over the same volume — the pattern
# deploy/derive.sh and deploy/value-snapshots.sh already use.
#
# Landing stays OFF unless something asks for it. To turn it on for an instance:
#   ~/.config/systemd/user/pkdump-refresh@<instance>.service.d/lake.conf
#   [Service]
#   Environment=PKDUMP_LAND_RAW=1
# The wrapper forwards it into the container. See deploy/LAKE.md §4.
# ── EXIT STATUS, AND WHY 2 STILL PAGES (pd-nons) ────────────────────────────
#   0  every upstream was acquired.
#   2  PARTIAL: the pokemontcg.io tail failed after exhausting its retries, so
#      the set list is as old as the last run that finished one — but the run
#      CONTINUED, and TCGCSV (a day's prices, which cannot be fetched later)
#      was acquired and the catalog derived from it.
#   1  the run failed.
#
# There is deliberately NO SuccessExitStatus=2 here, unlike
# pkdump-value-snapshots@.service. That job writes N tenant databases and "some
# of them" is a normal night; its wrapper names the skipped tenants and pushes
# its own warning, so a status systemd calls success is still reported. A tail
# that fails every night is a catalog whose set list has silently stopped
# advancing, and OnFailure= above is the only thing on this box that would ever
# say so.
#
# That argument held, and its THRESHOLD did not. Until 2026-08-16 exit 2 reached
# systemd verbatim, so a single upstream 502 paged: pokemontcg.io returned one on
# 2026-08-15 and the push arrived at 06:05 about someone else's API, with nothing
# to do but read it. Ryan had stopped reading the channel by then, which is how a
# real outage went unnoticed the same week.
#
# The signal is unchanged; deploy/refresh.sh now decides WHEN it fires. It keeps
# a .refresh-last-tail-ok marker on the data volume, swallows a partial run while
# a recent one succeeded, and exits 2 — paging exactly as before — once the set
# list has genuinely stopped advancing. Still no SuccessExitStatus=2: when the
# wrapper exits 2 now, it means the stall this unit was written to catch.
#
# What changed with pd-nons is not how loud a bad night is. It is that the
# night is no longer LOST: the tail used to run first and abort everything, so
# a single 5xx on /v2/sets meant no prices were imported at all.
#
#
# PKDUMP_HTTP_RETRY_ATTEMPTS / PKDUMP_HTTP_RETRY_BASE_MS (see
# crates/pkdump-ingest/src/retry.rs) widen the retry budget without a rebuild, for a
# stretch of bad upstream weather. Unset is 4 attempts at 500ms doubling.
#
# Set them the same way as PKDUMP_LAND_RAW above — a drop-in Environment= — because
# deploy/refresh.sh forwards them into the container. (Before pd-kncd this unit was a
# `podman exec`, which drops the caller's environment, so a drop-in reached the systemd
# unit and never reached the refresh. That is exactly the bug pd-kncd fixed, and it is
# why this paragraph no longer says "put it on the exec".)
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'exec bash {{REPO_DIR}}/deploy/refresh.sh %i'
TimeoutStartSec=1800
